Which brings up a YANI for one of the NetHack features that
irritates me so much there's surely a patch for it already,
but I'd like more.

I play on a MS-Windows system and my game data files are
identified by my user-id on that system (which happens to be
Owner-Xanthian, but at least the Windows port of NetHack is
smart enough to omit the "Owner-" part in greeting my PC
during the game) .

But that is really confounding two ideas: who owns the save
file, for which that is the correct answer, versus what
should be the name of the player character, which it seems
more than a bit vain to have always named for me!

I get around this by having several startup icons each of
which sets the user name on the command line, (so I get
file names prefixed "Owner-Dolan" or whatever, instead)  but
this is also an ugly solution; creating one for each
possible role/race/gender/alignment would exhaust my screen
space.

Modifying something each time I run NetHack is also an
ugly solution; at the rate I burn through PCs, I'd spend
half my time trying to think up new names.

That stuff that should be set and forget, but out of the way
and in as much detail as the user needs is exactly the sort
of information that belongs in a startup file.

I'd like to see the player character name be something that is set in
the startup defaults text file (defaults.nh in the
MS-Windows port), to as great a level of detail as possible,
something vaguely like:


OPTIONS=character_name_when_playing_as:role==barbarian&race==*&gender==male&alignment==*?Brak
the Barbarian Barber of Seville


OPTIONS=character_name_when_playing_as:role==barbarian&race==*&gender==female&alignment==*?Suzi
the Sucker Shearer of Seville


OPTIONS=character_name_when_playing_as:role==healer&race==*&gender==male&alignment==*?Matt
Malpractice

and so on.

Which would break save file compatiblity among the least of
its sins (character name fields should be longer, etc.)

That brings up a second YANI, using net neutral data
transmission protocols to make save files compatible between
systems, while isolating the few necessary incompatibilities
(how you spell a file-pathname among Macs, MS-Windows,
Amigas, and Unix/Linux, for one) to those same system
specific defaults files.  There surely isn't any _good_
excuse not to have hearse files for the same NetHack
release compatible among all platforms at this point in the
improvement of software science. One could even use XML,
though the data file size bloat would perhaps be horrific.

And most of all, as a third YANI, when Brak becomes female
due to an unfortunate collision with "you feel like a new",
I'd like Brak's name to change to Suzi's without any extra
effort on my part, since playing a male character under a
female name or vice versa grits the teeth of someone raised
in my generation (luckily we are all due to die off soon,
leaving the world a healthier place for our absence).

xanthian, as usual suggesting work I don't intend to do
